#N canvas 20 20 5200 8920 12;
#X text 20 20 phase6-bench -- the Phase 6 acceptance run: the Launchpad \, the grid arbiter \, the mode bus and the first c_clock instance. Ten seconds a step \, so you can watch the surface and mark each one off. Load it as a THIRD patch after mother.pd and main.pd. It touches nothing in the deployed patch: it only pushes onto mode \, disp \, err \, tempo \, start \, stop and panic \, exactly as a controller would., f 110;
#X text 20 130 RUN IN THE FOREGROUND AND WATCH THE LAUNCHPAD \, not the screen. Every step prints what it is sending and a PASS IF line BEFORE the surface moves -- including the steps whose correct result is that NOTHING happens \, which are otherwise impossible to mark off. Steps 12 \, 13 and 15 need your hands: nothing but the real controllers can exercise notein and ctlin., f 110;
#X text 20 240 NO COMMAS OR SEMICOLONS IN A PASS IF LINE. Both are message separators \, so one comma splits the string and the remainder lands somewhere unhelpful. phase3-bench says so \, phase4-bench was caught by it anyway \, and this file is generated partly so that it cannot happen again., f 110;
#X text 20 350 THE BEAT COUNTER BELOW is the one automated assertion here. Everything else about a grid is visual by nature -- there is no way to read back what the LEDs are actually showing \, so this bench proves the cases it contains and nothing more. Two of Phase 5's worst bugs were invisible to a bench that passed., f 110;
#X obj 4600 240 print;
#X obj 4600 180 r \$0-say;
#X obj 200 470 r clock;
#X obj 200 520 t b;
#X obj 200 570 f;
#X obj 340 620 + 1;
#X obj 340 670 t f f;
#X obj 340 720 f;
#X obj 560 520 r \$0-zero;
#X msg 560 570 0;
#X obj 200 720 r \$0-read;
#X obj 340 780 print BEATS;
#X text 700 470 Counts master beats. Zeroed and read by the tempo steps \, so the two printed numbers are ten seconds at 120 BPM and ten at 240 -- about 20 and about 40. On the Mac with DSP OFF both read 0 \, which looks exactly like a dead clock rather than a setting., f 70;
#X obj 20 900 loadbang;
#X obj 20 960 del 6000;
#X obj 200 1140 t b b b b b b;
#X msg 500 1210 === STEP-01-of-16 === baseline -- 120 BPM -- stopped -- compose mode-1;
#X obj 500 1270 s \$0-say;
#X msg 500 1340 PASS IF: the top row shows ONE bright green lamp at the far left and five dim ones beside it -- and the BOTTOM row of pads has a single white pad. Everything else on the surface is dark;
#X obj 500 1400 s \$0-say;
#X msg 3600 1210 120;
#X obj 3600 1270 s tempo;
#X msg 4120 1210 bang;
#X obj 4120 1270 s stop;
#X msg 4640 1210 compose mode-1;
#X obj 4640 1270 s mode;
#X obj 200 1480 del 10000;
#X obj 200 1610 t b b b b;
#X msg 500 1680 === STEP-02-of-16 === MODE -- selecting the fourth mode;
#X obj 500 1740 s \$0-say;
#X msg 500 1810 PASS IF: the bright lamp MOVES to the fourth position and the other five go dim. Nothing else on the grid changes;
#X obj 500 1870 s \$0-say;
#X msg 3600 1680 perform mode-4;
#X obj 3600 1740 s mode;
#X obj 200 1950 del 10000;
#X obj 200 2080 t b b b b;
#X msg 500 2150 === STEP-03-of-16 === MODE -- the sixth and last lamp;
#X obj 500 2210 s \$0-say;
#X msg 500 2280 PASS IF: the bright lamp lands on the sixth and last position. ONE MESSAGE PER STEP FOR ANYTHING VISUAL: two sent back to back both land inside the same frame and only the second is ever drawn -- found by running this bench and watching the painted frames rather than the surface;
#X obj 500 2340 s \$0-say;
#X msg 3600 2150 perform mode-6;
#X obj 3600 2210 s mode;
#X obj 200 2420 del 10000;
#X obj 200 2550 t b b b b;
#X msg 500 2620 === STEP-04-of-16 === MODAL -- the whole surface claimed;
#X obj 500 2680 s \$0-say;
#X msg 500 2750 PASS IF: EVERY pad and every lamp on the top row turns blue. The mode lamps are covered too -- that is the point of a modal. THE OLED MUST NOT CHANGE;
#X obj 500 2810 s \$0-say;
#X msg 3600 2620 grid modal 45;
#X obj 3600 2680 s disp;
#X obj 200 2890 del 10000;
#X obj 200 3020 t b b b b;
#X msg 500 3090 === STEP-05-of-16 === MODE CHANGE UNDERNEATH A MODAL -- nothing should be visible;
#X obj 500 3150 s \$0-say;
#X msg 500 3220 PASS IF: NOTHING HAPPENS. The surface stays blue. The mode really does change underneath and you will see it two steps from now;
#X obj 500 3280 s \$0-say;
#X msg 3600 3090 perform mode-3;
#X obj 3600 3150 s mode;
#X obj 200 3360 del 10000;
#X obj 200 3490 t b b b b;
#X msg 500 3560 === STEP-06-of-16 === MODAL OFF;
#X obj 500 3620 s \$0-say;
#X msg 500 3690 PASS IF: the grid returns to mode lamps and the beat row -- and the bright lamp is now the THIRD one -- which is the change made while the modal covered it;
#X obj 500 3750 s \$0-say;
#X msg 3600 3560 grid modal-off;
#X obj 3600 3620 s disp;
#X obj 200 3830 del 10000;
#X obj 200 3960 t b b b b;
#X msg 500 4030 === STEP-07-of-16 === ALERT -- a fail -- which outranks everything;
#X obj 500 4090 s \$0-say;
#X msg 500 4160 PASS IF: the whole surface turns RED -- and then goes back to the mode lamps BY ITSELF after about two seconds. A grid that stays red is the bug this step exists for;
#X obj 500 4220 s \$0-say;
#X msg 3600 4030 fail u_bench boom;
#X obj 3600 4090 s err;
#X obj 200 4300 del 10000;
#X obj 200 4430 t b b b b;
#X msg 500 4500 === STEP-08-of-16 === ALERT -- a warn -- which the grid must ignore;
#X obj 500 4560 s \$0-say;
#X msg 500 4630 PASS IF: NOTHING HAPPENS ON THE GRID. The OLED still shows the warning. Only a fail is worth the whole surface;
#X obj 500 4690 s \$0-say;
#X msg 3600 4500 warn u_bench quiet;
#X obj 3600 4560 s err;
#X obj 200 4770 del 10000;
#X obj 200 4900 t b b b b;
#X msg 500 4970 === STEP-09-of-16 === TRANSPORT -- start;
#X obj 500 5030 s \$0-say;
#X msg 500 5100 PASS IF: the white pad WALKS along the bottom row -- twice a second -- and the aux button goes green. On the Mac with DSP off it will not move -- tick enable-DSP first;
#X obj 500 5160 s \$0-say;
#X msg 3600 4970 bang;
#X obj 3600 5030 s start;
#X obj 200 5240 del 10000;
#X obj 200 5370 t b b b b;
#X msg 500 5440 === STEP-10-of-16 === TEMPO -- 240 BPM -- so the beat row should double;
#X obj 500 5500 s \$0-say;
#X msg 500 5570 PASS IF: the white pad moves twice as fast. The console prints BEATS for the ten seconds before this step and after it -- expect about 20 then about 40;
#X obj 500 5630 s \$0-say;
#X msg 3600 5440 240;
#X obj 3600 5500 s tempo;
#X obj 200 5710 del 10000;
#X obj 200 5840 t b b b b b;
#X msg 500 5910 === STEP-11-of-16 === back to 120 and stopped;
#X obj 500 5970 s \$0-say;
#X msg 500 6040 PASS IF: the beat row slows to two a second. THE CLOCK KEEPS RUNNING WHEN THE TRANSPORT STOPS -- so the pad must keep walking after the stop;
#X obj 500 6100 s \$0-say;
#X msg 3600 5910 120;
#X obj 3600 5970 s tempo;
#X msg 4120 5910 bang;
#X obj 4120 5970 s stop;
#X obj 200 6180 del 10000;
#X obj 200 6310 t b b b;
#X msg 500 6380 === STEP-12-of-16 === HANDS ON THE LAUNCHPAD -- press pads and ring buttons;
#X obj 500 6440 s \$0-say;
#X msg 500 6510 PASS IF: every pad you press reports pad-NN on the OLED with its velocity -- and a ring button reports lp-cc-NN. Pressure on a held pad reports NOTHING on the OLED -- that is deliberate and it is not a fault;
#X obj 500 6570 s \$0-say;
#X obj 200 6650 del 10000;
#X obj 200 6780 t b b b;
#X msg 500 6850 === STEP-13-of-16 === HANDS ON THE NANOKONTROL -- the six transport keys;
#X obj 500 6910 s \$0-say;
#X msg 500 6980 PASS IF: each of the six keys moves the bright lamp to its own position. This is the mode bus finally having a driver;
#X obj 500 7040 s \$0-say;
#X obj 200 7120 del 10000;
#X obj 200 7250 t b b b b;
#X msg 500 7320 === STEP-14-of-16 === PANIC -- the surface goes back to the device;
#X obj 500 7380 s \$0-say;
#X msg 500 7450 PASS IF: the Launchpad leaves Programmer Mode and its OWN display returns. The grid is no longer ours. KNOWN AND DELIBERATE: it stays that way until the patch is reloaded;
#X obj 500 7510 s \$0-say;
#X msg 3600 7320 bang;
#X obj 3600 7380 s panic;
#X obj 200 7590 del 10000;
#X obj 200 7720 t b b b;
#X msg 500 7790 === STEP-15-of-16 === HANDS -- press a pad now -- after the panic;
#X obj 500 7850 s \$0-say;
#X msg 500 7920 PASS IF: NOTHING REACHES THE OLED. In a stock layout the notes are musical pitches rather than r*10+c -- and decoding them as coordinates would publish nonsense. Reload the patch to get the grid back;
#X obj 500 7980 s \$0-say;
#X obj 200 8060 del 10000;
#X obj 200 8190 t b b b;
#X msg 500 8260 === STEP-16-of-16 === done -- stop the patch;
#X obj 500 8320 s \$0-say;
#X msg 500 8390 PASS IF: nothing further prints. Reload with deploy.sh to restore normal operation;
#X obj 500 8450 s \$0-say;
